      <Placemark>
        <name>Kyo-hwa-so No. 6, Sariwon
North Hwanghae Province</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AAjQbR5K3JyS6lWuID-sirhLPd38-SfrBbooFnolyRVu83V_EDS2YRRE_mjOMpySNDVo7y-ZSdDNbCHpuMKY0DC88o1N6MP0DcedApe-8s0eoEVG-Ofvc24F-Kdz2c0FAs0W2hTQud9feeltm7nBvAtN8fzOKzUns3NWiEEXD_BYEeVXW9LN4Wa52FReFCt24wvcywT2h3EDZCCPf6k?fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Sariwon City Prison, Sariwon, North Hwanghae Province, 38° 30’45.41”N 125° 46’26.59”E.<br><br>Estimated # of Prisoners: 3,000 to 4,000<br><br>Type of prisoners: Men, political prisoners<br><br>Type of Labor: Clothing and Shoe factory, farming, "model detention facility" <br><br>Kyo-hwa-so No. 6, Sariwon was one of the earliest North Korean prison camps to become widely known about outside of North Korea because of the involvement of Amnesty International in the release of Ali Lameda and Jacques Sedillot. They were members in good standing in the Venezuelan and French, respectively, communist parties. In 1967, both were recruited to Pyongyang by the North Korean Foreign Ministry to translate the writings of Kim Il-sung into Spanish and French. Lameda was a poet and author whose writings were well known throughout Latin America.<br><br>A second location associated with this camp, a potential 'model detention facility' is located in Osu-ri at 38° 29’0.94”N 125° 49’3.31”E.<br><br>Detailed analysis of Kyo-hwa-so No. 6, Sariwon can be found in this 2017 HRNK report: https://www.hrnk.org/uploads/pdfs/Hawk_The_Parallel_Gulag_Web.pdf<br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AAjQbR6EOQBNdNB6CBGXPxfPexJwl9vyyosB09-kvmcjgkaK-gbOImkA6RiP5IzNfL0V7pMoJAivdA266YCPtssjV2Io5wO_PfGyxqQJENAc8loU-0Rh8nhazo4L2wU5Co3ep2KH-rWTEP03o3WXLYA1ZVJQrVf0AkPAxh46Aeq_dRA82b4rv2ADqEY9H2Eb9Mop_sbG2HY_jMwhXt4?fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-959-F8971B</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
